# The text editor: `edit FILE` opens the file in a window of its own.
# Ctrl+S saves, Ctrl+Q leaves; the keyboard types in the editor's window.
@usb kbd
$ layout us
$ echo first line > /note.txt
$ edit /note.txt
editing /note.txt in a new window: Ctrl+S saves, Ctrl+Q leaves
# The window is on top (a blue title bar), with a status line (light gray)
# above the text (black).
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 300,36=3a6ea5 660,64=aaaaaa 660,200=000000
$ threads
~ editor     waiting

# Type at the end of the first line and on a new one, then save and leave.
@keys end spc p l u s ret s e c o n d ctrl+s
$ sleep 1
$ cat /note.txt
first line plus
second
@keys ctrl+q
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 300,36=a8d0f0 1500,78=3a6ea5
$ threads
!~ editor

# Leaving with changes that were not saved takes Ctrl+Q twice; the file stays as it was.
$ edit note.txt
editing /note.txt in a new window: Ctrl+S saves, Ctrl+Q leaves
@keys x x x ctrl+q
$ sleep 1
$ threads
~ editor     waiting
@keys ctrl+q
@screenshot 1920x1080 pixels 300,36=a8d0f0
$ cat /note.txt
first line plus
second

# A new file, in a folder; the close box leaves too (nothing is saved by it).
$ mkdir /docs
$ cd /docs
$ edit new.txt
editing /docs/new.txt in a new window: Ctrl+S saves, Ctrl+Q leaves
@keys h i ctrl+s ctrl+q
$ sleep 1
$ cat /docs/new.txt
hi

# What cannot be edited.
$ edit /docs
edit /docs: is a directory
$ edit
usage: edit FILE
$ desktop off
$ edit /note.txt
edit /note.txt: windows need the desktop (see desktop)
